 ## Overview
-Opacity compensation methods are techniques used in volume rendering to correct inaccuracies introduced by approximations in splatting or compositing, ensuring that the final image accurately represents the underlying data density and structure [S1, unofficial]. These methods solve the problem of premature occlusion or incorrect surface appearance by adjusting the opacity contribution of individual samples based on specific criteria such as value proximity, depth, or statistical uncertainty. Unlike standard volume rendering which applies a fixed transfer function, these methods dynamically modulate opacity to preserve internal features or enhance surface clarity [S2, S3, unofficial].
